@@s001 Lines, planes and hyperplanes
@@s002 ok:true: the point is on the line,no:false: the point is off
@@s003 due:w (normal vector),ok:points with w · x = b,dato:any point
@@s004 ok:the plane (the model),dato:intercept,ora:slope along x₁,due:slope along x₂
@@s005 ok:the points with w · x = b
@@s006 no:w · x − b > 0,ok:w · x − b < 0,ora:w · x − b = 0
@@s007 due:linear f(x) = 2x,ok:affine g(x) = 2x + 1
@@s008 dato:inputs (pupil),ora:weights,ok:outputs (screen)
@@s009 Lines, planes and hyperplanes
@@s010 Linear models are lines, planes and, with many features, hyperplanes. Here: what "the points that satisfy an equation" means, why a line is an inner product equal to a value, how to read a plane, why a hyperplane divides space in two, the difference between linear and affine, and the case with several outputs. Slides 53–57 of lecture 1 and 7–17 of lecture 2.
@@s011 The equation of a line: the points that make it true
@@s012 An equation with two unknowns, such as <span class="m">y = 2x + 1</span>, does not have a single solution: it is a <b>condition</b> that some pairs (x, y) satisfy and others do not. The line is the set of all the pairs that make it true (slide 8 of lecture 2: "all points satisfying the equation").
@@s013 To check whether a point lies on the line you substitute and see whether the equality holds. (1, 3): 2·1 + 1 = 3, true. (2, 4): 2·2 + 1 = 5 ≠ 4, false: the point lies below.
@@s014 Read with ML eyes, a model y = w₀ + w₁x is the set of points that the model "approves". The real data lie above or below; the vertical distance from that set is the error.
@@s015 True or false, point by point
@@s016 The line as an inner product: w · x = b
@@s017 Moving all the unknowns to the left, y = 2x + 1 becomes <span class="m">−2x₁ + x₂ = 1</span> (with x₁ = x and x₂ = y). The left-hand side is the inner product between <span class="m">w = [−2, 1]</span> and <span class="m">x = [x₁, x₂]</span>:
@@s018 This is the definition of <b>linear equation</b> from the slides: "linear equations in x are those that can be represented by an inner product" (slide 17 of lecture 2). Each input is multiplied by a number, then summed.
@@s019 The vector w has a geometric meaning: it is <b>perpendicular</b> to the line, and is called the <b>normal vector</b>. The reason: two points of the line have the same value b, so their difference d satisfies w · d = 0, that is it is orthogonal to w. Changing b moves the line parallel to itself.
@@s020 −2x₁ + x₂ = 1 and its normal vector
@@s021 The plane: an affine function of two variables
@@s022 With two features the regression model is <span class="m">y = w₀ + w₁x₁ + w₂x₂</span>. To draw it three axes are needed: two for the inputs, one for the output. The graph is a <b>plane</b> (slide 54 of lecture 1: "planes, affine multivariate function").
@@s023 The three parameters are read as in the line. <b>w₀</b> is the height of the plane above the origin. <b>w₁</b> is the slope in the direction of x₁: how much y changes if x₁ increases by 1 and x₂ stays still. <b>w₂</b> is the slope in the direction of x₂. A large weight means that feature influences the prediction a lot; a weight close to 0 means it matters little.
@@s024 Learning the model means finding the plane that passes as close as possible to a cloud of points in 3D: the same idea as the line, with one more parameter.
@@s025 y = 1 + 0.5x₁ + 0.3x₂
@@s026 Hyperplanes in n dimensions
@@s027 The equation <span class="m">w₁x₁ + w₂x₂ + … + wₙxₙ = b</span>, that is <span class="m">w · x = b</span>, describes an object with <b>one dimension less</b> than the space it lives in: in a line (n = 1) a point, in the plane (n = 2) a line, in space (n = 3) a plane. For any n it is called a <b>hyperplane</b> (<i>hyperplane</i>).
@@s028 A hyperplane in 50 dimensions cannot be imagined, but it is handled very well: it is still "an inner product equal to a value", w is still its normal vector, and all the formulas are computed component by component.
@@s029 Why it matters: the graph of a linear model with n features is a hyperplane in n + 1 dimensions (n inputs plus the output), and the boundary of a linear classifier is a hyperplane in the space of the inputs.
@@s030 w · x = b with 1, 2 and 3 inputs
@@s031 The two sides of a hyperplane
@@s032 A hyperplane divides space into <b>two halves</b>. To know on which side a point x lies you do not need to draw anything: the <b>sign</b> of <span class="m">w · x − b</span> is enough. Positive on one side (the one towards which w points), negative on the other, zero exactly on the hyperplane.
@@s033 This is why hyperplanes are so important in classification: a <b>linear classifier</b> learns w and b, and assigns the class by the sign. The hyperplane becomes the <b>decision boundary</b> (<i>decision boundary</i>), and the value |w · x − b| / ‖w‖ says how far the point is from the boundary, that is how "sure" the decision is (week 10, SVM).
@@s034 The sign of w · x − b
@@s035 Linear or affine?
@@s036 In mathematics a function is <b>linear</b> if it is written <span class="m">f(x) = w · x</span>, with no constant. Two consequences: it passes through the origin (f(0) = 0) and respects sums and multiples, f(a + b) = f(a) + f(b) and f(c·a) = c·f(a).
@@s037 With one constant more, <span class="m">g(x) = w · x + b</span>, the function is called <b>affine</b>: it is a linear function shifted. The regression line y = w₀ + w₁x is affine, and indeed the slides call it <i>affine mapping</i>.
@@s038 In ML language it is nevertheless called "linear model": what counts is that it is <b>linear in the parameters</b>. The trick to get back into the truly linear case is to add to the vector x a fixed component equal to 1: then w₀ becomes a weight like the others (it is the column of 1s of the design matrix, week 4, and the idea of homogeneous coordinates, week 5).
@@s039 f(x) = 2x versus g(x) = 2x + 1
@@s040 More outputs: one hyperplane per output
@@s041 When the model must predict <b>several numbers</b>, such as the two coordinates of the gaze on the screen, it is a vector-valued function (slide 57 of lecture 1). The simplest way to build it: a <b>separate</b> linear model for each output, each with its own weights.
@@s042 With two outputs there are two vectors of weights. Placed one above the other, as rows, they form a <b>matrix</b> W, and the two predictions are computed together with a matrix-vector product: <span class="m">ŷ = W x + b</span>. It is exactly the step from week 2 (vectors) to week 3 (matrices), and the form of the affine model of Assignment 1.
@@s043 From the pupil to the screen: two outputs, two hyperplanes
